  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-37149",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14201841/assets/06660df896e67cef560398ca24217cdba2e2b9a34fe40f1829b3e1d5d2db24e3.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,-6)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,8),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,8).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,8) & =2+6(-2)-3(8)+(-2)(8) \\\\  & =-50 \\\\ f(1,2) & =2+6(1)-3(2)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,8) & =2+6(2)-3(8)+(2)(8) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-50$ at the point $(-2,8),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6$ at the point $(2,8).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
